1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It's one of the world's most affordable sources of protein.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Hens lay 80 billion a year in the U.S. alone.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:14,000
You're looking at a bird that's producing about an egg every day or every other day.

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Grade A. Double A. Cage-free. Free-range.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Pasture-raised. Organic. Omega-3.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
White, brown, green, purple, black, large, extra-large.

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Incredibly large. Incredibly tiny. Powdered. And liquid.

8
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:31,960
bag, bottled, and in tankers.

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,460
They're lean, lean, egg-boggling machines. They're just great.

10
00:00:36,460 --> 00:00:41,000
If we cook them right, chug them wrong, or even sculpt their shells,

11
00:00:41,660 --> 00:00:44,260
they're incredible and edible.

12
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:48,100
Now, The Egg on Modern Marvels.

13
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:06,651
Eggs. The reproductive cells from which all life forms evolve, including us.

14
00:01:06,652 --> 00:01:07,960
So which came

15
00:01:07,961 --> 00:01:10,302
first, the chicken or the egg?

16
00:01:10,303 --> 00:01:15,220
The first eggs were laid by sea creatures in the ocean hundreds

17
00:01:15,221 --> 00:01:20,764
of millions of years ago. In water, the eggs needed no shells for protection.

18
00:01:20,765 --> 00:01:21,700
With reptiles

19
00:01:21,701 --> 00:01:28,651
came soft, leathery sacks. Dinosaurs laid the first hard-shelled eggs.

20
00:01:28,652 --> 00:01:30,240
Later, but still

21
00:01:30,241 --> 00:01:37,143
a hundred million years ago, the first birds laid their eggs in nests.

22
00:01:37,144 --> 00:01:38,820
So, the egg wins.

23
00:01:38,821 --> 00:01:43,780
Because Gallus domesticus, the chicken as we know it, is only four or five thousand

24
00:01:43,781 --> 00:01:50,567
years old. Today, its eggs are a major global food source.

25
00:01:50,568 --> 00:01:53,960
Welcome to the egg capital of

26
00:01:53,961 --> 00:02:04,267
America, Iowa, where every year 50 million hens lay more than 12 billion eggs.

27
00:02:04,268 --> 00:02:05,060
In the

28
00:02:05,061 --> 00:02:11,110
entire U.S., there are as many ladybirds as people, 280 million.

29
00:02:11,111 --> 00:02:13,000
Coincidentally, each

30
00:02:13,001 --> 00:02:19,280
Each American eats about the same number of eggs a single chicken puts out, 250 per year.

31
00:02:19,281 --> 00:02:24,860
So in effect, every person has a hen with his or her name on it.

32
00:02:24,861 --> 00:02:28,760
There are more than 200 breeds of chickens.

33
00:02:28,761 --> 00:02:33,240
But the classic white leghorn, originally bred in Italy, is the choice for most egg

34
00:02:33,241 --> 00:02:34,960
producers.

35
00:02:34,961 --> 00:02:41,000
Too small to be considered a meaty bird, it's built to lay eggs.

36
00:02:41,001 --> 00:02:46,700
This mega chicken coop in Stewart, Iowa is part of Rose Acre Farms, the nation's second

37
00:02:46,701 --> 00:02:49,100
largest egg producer.

38
00:02:49,101 --> 00:02:56,520
Every day, 1.5 million chickens housed here lay more than 800,000 eggs.

39
00:02:56,521 --> 00:02:59,460
The process begins at hatcheries.

40
00:02:59,461 --> 00:03:03,720
Because leghorn hens are cannibalistic and may literally peck each other to death in

41
00:03:03,721 --> 00:03:08,800
close quarters, the sharp tips of their beaks are trimmed by machine within two days of

42
00:03:08,801 --> 00:03:09,800
birth.

43
00:03:09,801 --> 00:03:14,700
A process which egg producers say is painless, similar to trimming a fingernail.

44
00:03:16,420 --> 00:03:22,300
Because these leg horns are intended solely for egg production, male chicks are killed within a day or two of hatching,

45
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:27,040
using procedures approved by the American Medical Veterinary Association.

46
00:03:29,560 --> 00:03:35,740
At an age of approximately 18 weeks, hens are ready to lay and are moved into production houses.

47
00:03:35,741 --> 00:03:43,080
We're in house three right now, which we call 3A and B.

48
00:03:43,440 --> 00:03:45,580
A stands for attic, B stands for basement.

49
00:03:46,540 --> 00:03:51,360
This cage system here is eight high with a floor in the middle.

50
00:03:52,260 --> 00:03:56,500
A typical hen house at Rose Acre is built around 400-foot rows,

51
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,380
with cages stacked eight high on each side.

52
00:04:00,100 --> 00:04:05,020
Every building has eight rows, housing 200 to 250,000 hens.

53
00:04:05,740 --> 00:04:13,220
The Stewart, Iowa facility has six houses, accounting for the total chicken population of up to 1.5 million.

54
00:04:15,540 --> 00:04:18,120
Each cage houses five to seven chickens.

55
00:04:19,460 --> 00:04:26,860
The current industry guideline requires a minimum of 67 square inches of space per bird, about half a square foot.

56
00:04:26,861 --> 00:04:34,300
Feed is continually replenished in front of cages, water in the back, and manure is removed

57
00:04:34,301 --> 00:04:38,580
by large conveyor belts every sixth day.

58
00:04:38,581 --> 00:04:40,640
Eggs roll gently onto smaller belts.

59
00:04:40,641 --> 00:04:48,300
Once the egg rolls out onto the 4-inch belt, the egg gets rolled into the basket here.

60
00:04:48,301 --> 00:04:53,580
The basket lifts the egg up and carries it down to our main belt.

61
00:04:53,581 --> 00:04:59,520
the main belt as a mass counter which counts all the eggs coming out of this row.

62
00:04:59,521 --> 00:05:04,440
With freezing winter temperatures in Iowa, buildings are enclosed and use artificial

63
00:05:04,441 --> 00:05:07,920
light 16 to 17 hours per day.

64
00:05:07,921 --> 00:05:11,760
Chickens do sleep about 8 hours per night.

65
00:05:11,761 --> 00:05:17,220
The caged system accounts for nearly 98% of American egg production.

66
00:05:17,221 --> 00:05:21,640
Most eggs sold for $2 per dozen or less are produced this way.

67
00:05:21,641 --> 00:05:25,600
amazingly efficient process especially when you consider where each egg started

68
00:05:25,601 --> 00:05:31,640
in the first place. In this cutaway of the hen up here we'd be looking at the

69
00:05:31,641 --> 00:05:36,280
ovary the one functional ovary on the left hand side of the hen's body and

70
00:05:36,281 --> 00:05:41,680
this contains all the future egg yolks going from those that are microscopic in

71
00:05:41,681 --> 00:05:48,280
size to the next one that's going to be released. Inside a hen's oviduct each

72
00:05:48,281 --> 00:05:53,600
Each yolk is covered with a membrane, fibers, and the egg white, called albumin.

73
00:05:53,601 --> 00:05:59,760
The yolk rotates as it develops, twisting fibers into rope-like strands that anchor the yolk.

74
00:05:59,761 --> 00:06:06,100
The strands form the white stringy substance, often seen after cracking a fresh egg.

75
00:06:06,101 --> 00:06:10,960
The egg shell, a crystalline form of calcium carbonate, is deposited around the egg just

76
00:06:10,961 --> 00:06:14,360
before it's laid.

77
00:06:14,361 --> 00:06:18,880
At Roseacre Farms, when eggs leave the hen houses, they travel into grading facilities

78
00:06:18,881 --> 00:06:21,840
where they're inspected, sized, and prepared for market.

79
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:29,460
After passing through an automated egg wash, it's time for QC, quality control.

80
00:06:30,480 --> 00:06:34,040
What that person is doing is looking for the bad eggs.

81
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:41,140
Bad eggs meaning bloods, leakers, or really dirty eggs that will not get cleaned.

82
00:06:41,141 --> 00:06:47,380
What we got in here is six cameras that are pointed down on the eggs as they go through.

83
00:06:47,980 --> 00:06:58,380
Once the camera finds the dirt, pinpoints it, the computer remembers where it is on the roll, and it'll get rewashed automatically.

84
00:06:59,720 --> 00:07:04,840
Eggs then move across computer-controlled sensors that check for cracks and weigh the eggs.

85
00:07:04,841 --> 00:07:08,900
The computer will know what size it is, and then it'll say,

86
00:07:09,060 --> 00:07:13,240
okay, you are a medium, you're a large, you're an X, you're a jumbo.

87
00:07:13,920 --> 00:07:18,320
It'll know where to take it down the line to the carton where it needs to be packed.

88
00:07:21,400 --> 00:07:23,240
Here, eggs are also graded.

89
00:07:24,020 --> 00:07:27,600
Double A's have the smallest air cells, grade A's slightly larger,

90
00:07:28,340 --> 00:07:31,740
and grade B's larger still, with some discoloration.

91
00:07:31,741 --> 00:07:37,420
Finally, workers prepare thousands of cartons of fresh eggs for shipping.

92
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:43,560
Normal shelf life for refrigerated eggs is three to four weeks.

93
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:47,640
We're in the cooler right now. It's about 36 degrees in here.

94
00:07:48,020 --> 00:07:52,660
Where the eggs are stored for a couple days, or the day of they're being shipped out.

95
00:07:53,660 --> 00:07:57,380
We now return to The Egg on Modern Marbles.

96
00:07:57,381 --> 00:08:03,380
With efficient egg production comes environmental challenges.

97
00:08:03,381 --> 00:08:07,380
As manure dries, ammonia forms.

98
00:08:07,381 --> 00:08:09,380
Levels need to be carefully monitored.

99
00:08:09,381 --> 00:08:14,380
Too much ammonia can cause respiratory problems for birds and humans.

100
00:08:14,381 --> 00:08:18,380
Scientists from Iowa State University's Egg Industry Institute

101
00:08:18,381 --> 00:08:24,380
are working with Rose Acre to develop new ways to control ammonia emissions.

102
00:08:24,381 --> 00:08:28,320
We're inside what we call the mobile air emission monitoring unit.

103
00:08:28,321 --> 00:08:36,840
We basically have shown in the lab that different diets can affect the ammonia emission from laying hand manure.

104
00:08:37,920 --> 00:08:41,480
Hens are fed a diet of corn, soy meal, vitamins and minerals.

105
00:08:43,640 --> 00:08:49,680
In labs at Iowa State, variations of that diet are fed to chickens in controlled observation chambers

106
00:08:49,681 --> 00:08:54,680
to determine not only the effects physically, but psychologically.

107
00:08:56,380 --> 00:09:01,280
As a result of animal welfare concerns and legislation in Europe and the United States,

108
00:09:02,140 --> 00:09:05,320
alternatives to traditional caged production are becoming more common.

109
00:09:06,920 --> 00:09:10,360
Producers like Rose Acre have begun establishing cage-free facilities.

110
00:09:12,700 --> 00:09:16,020
Smaller companies like Petaluma Farms in Northern California

111
00:09:16,021 --> 00:09:18,800
have been cage-free for more than 20 years.

112
00:09:19,680 --> 00:09:26,680
Petaluma Farms routinely houses between 50 and 60,000 hens.

113
00:09:26,681 --> 00:09:32,680
Cage-free farms account for a small part of American egg production, less than 2%.

114
00:09:32,681 --> 00:09:36,680
The chickens have free choice all day long.

115
00:09:36,681 --> 00:09:39,680
These are the feeders right here.

116
00:09:39,681 --> 00:09:43,680
And then behind me are the nest boxes that we actually bring in from Europe.

117
00:09:43,681 --> 00:09:48,680
The environment of the nest boxes is a little dark and a little more secluded for the chickens,

118
00:09:48,681 --> 00:09:51,480
so she can feel a little protected when she wants to lay her egg,

119
00:09:51,481 --> 00:09:53,600
as opposed to being out in this open area.

120
00:09:53,601 --> 00:09:55,940
There's actually a belt that runs underneath them

121
00:09:55,941 --> 00:09:58,520
that once a day we kind of shoo all the chickens out

122
00:09:58,521 --> 00:10:00,020
and the eggs roll onto this belt.

123
00:10:03,020 --> 00:10:06,160
Chicken manure is handled differently at Petaluma Farms,

124
00:10:06,161 --> 00:10:10,440
where it's mixed into layers of litter on the chicken house floors.

125
00:10:10,441 --> 00:10:12,420
We're using a deep litter situation

126
00:10:12,421 --> 00:10:15,380
where we put in six to eight inches of rice holes on the floor

127
00:10:15,381 --> 00:10:18,260
so the birds can root around and dust themselves.

128
00:10:18,261 --> 00:10:23,020
And actually, on the bottom end of it, as the birds get older, it will eventually compost on its own.

129
00:10:23,720 --> 00:10:30,580
Like their counterparts at caged egg farms, Petaluma Farms' cage-free chickens still need to have their beaks clipped right after birth.

130
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:37,560
If we left that hook on the end, they would do real damage to each other when they were picking at each other, even to preen each other.

131
00:10:38,820 --> 00:10:46,880
Petaluma Farms also raises Rhode Island red chickens in an environment where roosters mingle with hens, producing brown-shelled fertile eggs.

132
00:10:48,260 --> 00:10:55,260
Embryos never develop in the eggs because they're refrigerated within a day of being laid.

133
00:10:55,261 --> 00:11:00,260
You might think a white egg comes from a white chicken and a brown egg from a brown chicken.

134
00:11:00,261 --> 00:11:02,260
Not necessarily.

135
00:11:02,261 --> 00:11:06,260
The white egg and the brown egg, the difference actually is the earlobe of the chicken.

136
00:11:06,261 --> 00:11:11,260
It's a sex link, so the earlobe, if it's white, it's going to be a white egg,

137
00:11:11,261 --> 00:11:14,260
and if it's brown, it's going to be a brown egg.

138
00:11:14,261 --> 00:11:17,920
Petaluma farm eggs are cleaned, graded, and prepared for market,

139
00:11:18,320 --> 00:11:23,700
with technology similar to that used by larger caged farms, but on a much smaller scale.

140
00:11:24,900 --> 00:11:27,640
Market prices range from $3 to $5 per dozen.

141
00:11:28,760 --> 00:11:31,780
But that's still not top dollar for eggs laid in America.

142
00:11:32,740 --> 00:11:36,320
The highest-price eggs come from some of the lowest-tech operations.

143
00:11:37,880 --> 00:11:42,560
Eat Well Farm in Dixon, California, is one of relatively few egg producers

144
00:11:42,561 --> 00:11:45,320
that allows chickens to pasture graze.

145
00:11:46,680 --> 00:11:48,980
British horticulturalist Nigel Walker

146
00:11:48,981 --> 00:11:51,200
moved to the U.S. 20 years ago

147
00:11:51,201 --> 00:11:53,940
and purchased land to produce organic vegetables

148
00:11:53,941 --> 00:11:55,260
and farm-fresh eggs

149
00:11:55,261 --> 00:11:57,640
laid by chickens who spend most of their days

150
00:11:57,641 --> 00:11:58,480
in the great outdoors.

151
00:11:59,920 --> 00:12:01,900
We don't even call us free-range

152
00:12:01,901 --> 00:12:04,100
because free-range can literally mean

153
00:12:04,101 --> 00:12:06,780
a huge barn with 20,000 chickens in

154
00:12:06,781 --> 00:12:07,840
with the door open.

155
00:12:08,640 --> 00:12:10,420
The chickens are not used to going out.

156
00:12:10,500 --> 00:12:11,320
They won't go out.

157
00:12:11,321 --> 00:12:13,060
These chickens go out all the time.

158
00:12:13,660 --> 00:12:19,780
We do everything to make it so the chickens have as calm and as idyllic life as possible.

159
00:12:20,640 --> 00:12:24,320
Nigel purchases his production red chickens from a local hatchery.

160
00:12:25,260 --> 00:12:27,080
But their beaks are not clipped.

161
00:12:27,940 --> 00:12:29,500
Two chickens get into a fight here.

162
00:12:30,280 --> 00:12:31,100
There's room to run.

163
00:12:32,320 --> 00:12:35,940
The hens spend most of their days grazing on natural grasses

164
00:12:35,941 --> 00:12:38,740
or eating organic blends of grain and corn.

165
00:12:38,741 --> 00:12:45,980
The hens lay their eggs and sleep in chicken coops built on mobile home frames.

166
00:12:45,981 --> 00:12:49,460
There's nest boxes inside where the chickens lay their eggs.

167
00:12:49,461 --> 00:12:51,900
There's roosts where they roost at night.

168
00:12:51,901 --> 00:12:55,680
There's hanging water drinker, nipple drinkers there.

169
00:12:55,681 --> 00:12:58,540
So they give some clean water 24-7.

170
00:12:58,541 --> 00:13:04,500
And then when the very hot days, when we get above 95 degrees, we have these misters, these

171
00:13:04,501 --> 00:13:06,500
tubes surrounding the houses.

172
00:13:06,501 --> 00:13:12,100
and basically it's fogs it i mean it's just like you know southern california patio party

173
00:13:12,980 --> 00:13:16,820
the mobile chicken coops are part of nigel's solution to the manure issue

174
00:13:18,580 --> 00:13:23,860
after the chickens have grazed a pasture for several months dropping manure as they go the

175
00:13:23,861 --> 00:13:30,660
coops and chickens are moved to a new pasture the vacated area having been naturally fertilized

176
00:13:30,661 --> 00:13:34,260
is then available to grow crops or regenerate pasture grasses.

177
00:13:36,980 --> 00:13:39,300
After a productive life of one to two years,

178
00:13:39,860 --> 00:13:43,300
Nigel's chickens also endure what he calls their unhappy day.

179
00:13:44,900 --> 00:13:48,340
When these chickens have finished two years of laying here,

180
00:13:48,341 --> 00:13:52,500
the third year they really don't lay enough eggs to pay for their feed,

181
00:13:52,501 --> 00:13:56,260
so they have to go, you know? We all have to pay our way.

182
00:13:56,261 --> 00:14:00,740
And what happens to them, we have a good demand for spent chickens,

183
00:14:00,920 --> 00:14:05,560
and they go to a slaughterhouse in Sacramento where they're processed for stewing chickens.

184
00:14:06,860 --> 00:14:12,520
Eatwell Farm eggs are sold only on a co-op basis with fresh organic produce to private customers.

185
00:14:14,060 --> 00:14:19,100
The eggs cost $8 per dozen, four times the cost of caged production eggs.

186
00:14:20,200 --> 00:14:22,940
But Nigel says that buyers can taste the difference.

187
00:14:22,941 --> 00:14:29,980
We deliver about 900 boxes of produce every week, and the customers get the eggs with their boxes,

188
00:14:30,560 --> 00:14:33,620
and we have 200 people waiting to get into the farm.

189
00:14:34,580 --> 00:14:39,120
And it can take anything from five, six, seven months to get a spot.

190
00:14:39,740 --> 00:14:41,180
And there's never enough eggs.

191
00:14:42,200 --> 00:14:43,380
That's just the reality.

192
00:14:44,700 --> 00:14:52,020
A hen requires five ounces of food, ten ounces of water, and 24 to 26 hours to produce one egg.

193
00:14:52,940 --> 00:14:55,740
30 minutes later, she starts all over again.

194
00:14:57,100 --> 00:14:59,620
The Egg will return on Modern Marvels.

195
00:15:01,180 --> 00:15:05,140
We now return to The Egg on Modern Marvels.

196
00:15:07,480 --> 00:15:11,320
Fresh eggs usually reach markets within two days of being laid.

197
00:15:12,320 --> 00:15:13,840
Then comes the shopper's dilemma.

198
00:15:14,380 --> 00:15:16,500
So many choices and confusing labels.

199
00:15:17,320 --> 00:15:18,320
What's an egg eater to do?

200
00:15:18,980 --> 00:15:21,100
Let's start with taste and nutritional value.

201
00:15:21,101 --> 00:15:29,980
I know people who pay several dollars more a dozen for the eggs because they

202
00:15:29,981 --> 00:15:34,700
don't want to buy eggs from hens who were kept in cages that's a philosophical

203
00:15:34,701 --> 00:15:39,700
decision and they have the ability to pay more for it and I think that's great

204
00:15:39,701 --> 00:15:44,320
in terms of nutritional value if there is no nutritional difference so the egg

205
00:15:44,321 --> 00:15:49,820
laid by a hand who is maintained in a cage is going to be same as the

206
00:15:49,821 --> 00:15:55,100
nutritional value from an egg laid by a hen that was on the ground.

207
00:15:55,101 --> 00:16:02,100
What can affect an eggs taste and nutrition however is the hen's diet.

208
00:16:02,101 --> 00:16:05,580
The term organic on a carton can make a difference.

209
00:16:05,581 --> 00:16:14,260
Typically the birds that are producing organic eggs they have to be fed a diet that's made

210
00:16:14,261 --> 00:16:19,460
up of grains that were also produced organically.

211
00:16:19,461 --> 00:16:23,280
Another significant egg carton term is omega-3 enhanced.

212
00:16:24,660 --> 00:16:30,260
Hens producing these eggs are fed a diet enriched with flaxseed, fish oil, or DHA algae

213
00:16:30,261 --> 00:16:33,820
to produce fatty acids that can help prevent heart disease.

214
00:16:34,740 --> 00:16:40,620
If the choices among eggs sold in cartons aren't complicated enough, add another decision.

215
00:16:41,460 --> 00:16:43,240
Shelled, liquid, or powder.

216
00:16:44,340 --> 00:16:48,360
More than a third of all eggs now leave producers without shells.

217
00:16:49,460 --> 00:16:56,460
Like its nearby sister farm in Stewart, Iowa, this Rose Acre facility in Guthrie Center

218
00:16:56,461 --> 00:16:59,460
houses hundreds of thousands of hens.

219
00:16:59,461 --> 00:17:05,460
But most eggs here are processed through the facility's breaking plan.

220
00:17:05,461 --> 00:17:10,460
As in Stewart, machinery in Guthrie cleans and sorts the eggs automatically.

221
00:17:10,461 --> 00:17:17,460
But checks or cracks are detected not by visual inspection, but sound waves.

222
00:17:17,461 --> 00:17:24,460
Eggs with imperfections, and many without depending on market demand, are cracked and separated by machine.

223
00:17:24,461 --> 00:17:34,460
It's the same technique a kitchen cook would use, but the machine does it a bit faster, at a rate of 28,000 eggs per hour.

224
00:17:34,461 --> 00:17:42,460
Each egg moves into a cracker, where a knife gently cracks the shell and splits it apart.

225
00:17:42,461 --> 00:17:49,000
The egg drops into a small container with an opening large enough for the white to separate and drip into a tray below.

226
00:17:51,300 --> 00:17:53,280
Incredibly, this is old technology.

227
00:17:54,680 --> 00:18:01,180
A newer egg breaker just installed does the same job at the rate of 108,000 eggs per hour.

228
00:18:02,520 --> 00:18:10,020
The shells, rich in calcium, are collected in containers and sold or given to area farmers to be used as crop fertilizer.

229
00:18:10,021 --> 00:18:15,700
They also show promise in their ability to absorb carbon dioxide in the production of hydrogen.

230
00:18:17,120 --> 00:18:22,520
And collagen, harvested from eggshell membranes, offers numerous commercial applications.

231
00:18:25,580 --> 00:18:31,880
Fresh from the breaker, the whites, yolks, and whole liquid eggs wait in giant chilled holding tanks

232
00:18:31,881 --> 00:18:35,540
for their next stop in the production line, pasteurization.

233
00:18:35,541 --> 00:18:41,540
The sweet spot for pasteurization is only a matter of 10 degrees.

234
00:18:41,541 --> 00:18:44,540
Hot, but not too hot please.

235
00:18:44,541 --> 00:18:50,540
Normally we gotta be above 140 degrees to get a good bacteria kill,

236
00:18:50,541 --> 00:18:56,540
but yeah, we don't want to get above 150 degrees or we'll start cooking egg inside that breast.

237
00:18:56,541 --> 00:18:59,540
It could get ugly pretty quick.

238
00:18:59,541 --> 00:19:06,540
Without their natural shells to protect them, liquid egg products require sanitary processing.

239
00:19:06,541 --> 00:19:11,540
Basically we're going through some checks to make sure everything is sterile.

240
00:19:11,541 --> 00:19:20,540
And the first thing that we do is we purchase the bags that are irradiated already to make sure there's no bacteria growing inside the bag.

241
00:19:20,541 --> 00:19:25,320
And then from there, the machine is kept sterile with steam.

242
00:19:25,540 --> 00:19:29,820
So the steam keeps the machine hot enough to inhibit bacteria growth.

243
00:19:30,420 --> 00:19:32,920
The liquid eggs are sent on their way in packaging,

244
00:19:33,180 --> 00:19:37,860
ranging from 160 egg bags to stainless steel tanker trucks,

245
00:19:38,280 --> 00:19:43,660
holding 48,000 pounds of liquid, more than 400,000 whites or yolks.

246
00:19:44,480 --> 00:19:48,780
The trucks are bound from manufacturers of products like baked goods or mayonnaise.

247
00:19:50,540 --> 00:19:55,160
The processing of liquid eggs gives them double the shelf life of refrigerated shelled eggs.

248
00:19:56,080 --> 00:19:57,360
Nearly two months.

249
00:19:58,700 --> 00:20:02,000
But some customers need even longer periods of guaranteed freshness.

250
00:20:03,540 --> 00:20:05,240
Enter the powdered egg.

251
00:20:06,280 --> 00:20:12,500
In another state-of-the-art machine, liquid becomes powder at a rate of 4,000 pounds per hour.

252
00:20:13,600 --> 00:20:16,700
Behind me, what we have is what we call an egg dryer.

253
00:20:16,701 --> 00:20:25,660
What it does is pressurize the liquid egg up to 3,000 PSI and spray that into this box dryer.

254
00:20:26,280 --> 00:20:29,840
Inside the box, it's about 440 degrees.

255
00:20:30,280 --> 00:20:37,080
Within six seconds, the liquid egg is turned into a powder before it hits the floor.

256
00:20:38,440 --> 00:20:44,620
Outside the dryer, powdered eggs are sifted and placed into 50-pound bags and boxes for shipping.

257
00:20:45,580 --> 00:20:49,320
The dried eggs are usually sold to institutions and ice cream producers.

258
00:20:51,160 --> 00:20:54,220
But liquid product has caught on with many consumers,

259
00:20:55,140 --> 00:20:58,860
especially those who want only egg whites to keep their cholesterol down.

260
00:21:00,460 --> 00:21:02,680
But are egg yolks really that bad for us?

261
00:21:04,380 --> 00:21:07,500
Fifteen years ago, eggs got a bad name.

262
00:21:08,380 --> 00:21:12,320
In particular, yolks were suddenly considered as unhealthy as fatty red meats,

263
00:21:12,321 --> 00:21:14,020
rich ice creams, and cheesecake.

264
00:21:14,620 --> 00:21:19,320
But that was then, and this is now.

265
00:21:19,321 --> 00:21:23,260
Research scientists in several departments at Iowa State University have studied the

266
00:21:23,261 --> 00:21:26,500
effect on the human body of eating whole eggs.

267
00:21:26,501 --> 00:21:32,380
They've all come to virtually the same conclusion as published health recommendations.

268
00:21:32,381 --> 00:21:36,980
The American Heart Association has set a recommendation of four eggs per week.

269
00:21:36,981 --> 00:21:42,240
Other groups will have that ramped up to about one egg a day or seven a week would be.

270
00:21:42,241 --> 00:21:48,800
So an egg a day is still considered to be fine for the average, for the normal healthy adult

271
00:21:48,801 --> 00:21:52,860
without any other factors of high cholesterol or heart disease.

272
00:21:52,861 --> 00:21:57,520
Other tests at Iowa State have involved feeding rats various sources of protein to determine

273
00:21:57,521 --> 00:22:02,320
just how well egg protein ranks in nutritional value.

274
00:22:02,321 --> 00:22:03,320
The verdict?

275
00:22:03,321 --> 00:22:08,920
You don't need to eat an expensive steak to get a protein fix when an egg selling for

276
00:22:08,921 --> 00:22:10,560
15 cents will do the job.

277
00:22:12,240 --> 00:22:15,380
Many eggs we consume every day we never even see.

278
00:22:16,220 --> 00:22:22,560
They're in cakes, pies, custards, meatloaves, mayonnaise, salad dressings, and even pasta.

279
00:22:23,700 --> 00:22:26,120
The Egg will return on Modern Marvels.

