from fresh beet juice.

427
00:33:37,025 --> 00:33:42,420
Twice a week, 36 bunches of beets are steamed and then immediately peeled.

428
00:33:42,421 --> 00:33:47,635
peeled. After it's peeled, we put them into this slicer.

429
00:33:47,636 --> 00:33:51,080
We slice it and goes right underneath

430
00:33:51,081 --> 00:33:57,800
where all the spices and the vinegar and it's ready to use it for pick on the eggs.

431
00:33:59,180 --> 00:34:04,020
600 hard-boiled eggs are soaked in the vinegar and spiced beet juice for three days.

432
00:34:04,940 --> 00:34:08,040
Then they move to the countertop where they stay fresh up to a week.

433
00:34:08,041 --> 00:34:14,500
Pickling in this case is intended to add flavor more than preservation.

434
00:34:14,501 --> 00:34:16,880
Some newbies need to be talked into trying one.

435
00:34:16,881 --> 00:34:18,500
I say, okay, try it.

436
00:34:18,501 --> 00:34:21,900
If you don't like it, you're not going to pay for it.

437
00:34:21,901 --> 00:34:22,900
It's on the house.

438
00:34:22,901 --> 00:34:29,200
Slice it and they taste it and they ask for another one.

439
00:34:29,201 --> 00:34:33,720
If you find the color purple intriguing, how about the color black?

440
00:34:33,721 --> 00:34:38,240
This is an Asian delicacy known as a thousand-year-old egg.

441
00:34:38,241 --> 00:34:42,400
It's also called a century egg or simply a preserved egg.

442
00:34:42,401 --> 00:34:44,760
It is not hard-boiled.

443
00:34:44,761 --> 00:34:50,800
It is merely packed in clay, ash, salt, lime and straw for up to a month.

444
00:34:50,801 --> 00:34:52,280
Not really a century.

445
00:34:52,281 --> 00:34:55,080
The cooking is caused by fermentation.

446
00:34:55,081 --> 00:35:01,940
At specialty restaurants like Typhoon in Santa Monica, California, the egg is simply

447
00:35:01,941 --> 00:35:09,800
sliced in half revealing a garish green yolk with a smell similar to ammonia and

448
00:35:09,801 --> 00:35:17,960
it's served over tofu some people say it smells like urine but when you taste it

449
00:35:17,961 --> 00:35:21,740
it that taste is not there that smell that you smell is not it take the taste

450
00:35:21,741 --> 00:35:27,900
you get it's a very creamy sort of soft it has a yolky flavor to but a very

451
00:35:27,901 --> 00:35:35,400
richer it's much richer than the normal chicken egg yolk upstairs from typhoon in the hump sushi

452
00:35:35,401 --> 00:35:42,900
bar chef kiyoshiro Yamamoto creates exotic egg dishes on a much smaller scale with quail eggs

453
00:35:42,901 --> 00:35:52,620
poached precisely at 140 degrees so only the yolk cooks not the white then he prepares his own

454
00:35:52,621 --> 00:35:54,620
spoon-sized omelets.

455
00:35:54,621 --> 00:35:58,620
The sea urchin, oysters, and the king crab,

456
00:35:58,621 --> 00:36:00,620
a Japanese mushroom.

457
00:36:00,621 --> 00:36:04,620
For a sushi chef, quail eggs aren't simply food.

458
00:36:04,621 --> 00:36:06,620
They're miniature works of art.

459
00:36:06,621 --> 00:36:10,620
The Chinese consume 40% of the world's egg production,

460
00:36:10,621 --> 00:36:14,620
nearly 400 billion eggs per year.

461
00:36:14,621 --> 00:36:18,620
The egg will return on Modern Marvels.

462
00:36:18,621 --> 00:36:22,840
We now return to the egg on Modern Marbles.

463
00:36:24,500 --> 00:36:28,740
The perfectly shaped form of the egg has inspired artists for centuries.

464
00:36:30,180 --> 00:36:34,900
These are Russia's famous Fabergé eggs from the early 1900s.

465
00:36:35,740 --> 00:36:38,660
Made from gold, marble, and jewels.

466
00:36:40,580 --> 00:36:44,780
Even simple Easter eggs have been elevated to innovative art.

467
00:36:44,781 --> 00:36:53,960
But few artists have had the patience and steady hand to perform work like eggshell sculptor

468
00:36:53,961 --> 00:36:57,260
Gary LeMaster of Iowa City.

469
00:36:57,261 --> 00:37:07,180
What began as a hobby 30 years ago has evolved into a life's passion.

470
00:37:07,181 --> 00:37:12,340
Working with an ostrich shell, Gary is able to carve layers of relief.

471
00:37:12,341 --> 00:37:17,260
Delicate power dental tools allow him to work within a custom-built case, which vacuums

472
00:37:17,261 --> 00:37:19,260
shell dust as he carves.

473
00:37:19,261 --> 00:37:27,960
I started just shaping things, taking off the outer layer.

474
00:37:27,961 --> 00:37:33,580
We create the illusion of depth because we're only working with about somewhere between

475
00:37:33,581 --> 00:37:39,200
a 32nd of an inch and maybe a 16th of an inch thick shell here.

476
00:37:39,201 --> 00:37:47,640
And so you always point the burr towards what element is going to remain or appear to be

477
00:37:47,641 --> 00:37:50,820
higher than the one next to it.

478
00:37:50,821 --> 00:37:56,180
This is a good place to show because we have part of a leaf here that is rolled over on

479
00:37:56,181 --> 00:38:05,920
itself and instead of having, being satisfied with just this, this sort of 90 degree angle

480
00:38:05,921 --> 00:38:11,280
here to show the depth will actually go in and undercut it, which will create even more

481
00:38:11,281 --> 00:38:18,420
of a shadow and therefore more of an illusion of depth.

482
00:38:18,421 --> 00:38:24,480
The natural colors of different eggs give Yeri even more creative opportunities.

483
00:38:24,481 --> 00:38:29,240
Beneath the green surface of an emu eggshell lie multi-color layers.

484
00:38:29,241 --> 00:38:35,260
There's no paint on here, which people traditionally think there is when they see them.

485
00:38:35,261 --> 00:38:40,820
When you're carving with an aggressive burr, usually you can get different color layers

486
00:38:40,821 --> 00:38:43,640
out from the egg.

487
00:38:43,641 --> 00:38:47,580
The white, which is the deepest, and it's just paper thin.

488
00:38:47,581 --> 00:38:52,100
The teal, which I'm working on now, and I think this is going to eventually go down

489
00:38:52,101 --> 00:38:53,880
to the white.

490
00:38:53,881 --> 00:38:57,580
And then, of course, the dark outer layer.

491
00:38:57,581 --> 00:39:04,920
This is a commission for someone in the great Pacific Northwest whose wife loves dolphins

492
00:39:04,921 --> 00:39:14,420
Prices for Gary's carved shells range from $100 for simple designs to several thousand for commissioned works.

493
00:39:17,200 --> 00:39:22,420
The lattice or lace-like carvings he calls filigree often draw the most attention.

494
00:39:25,940 --> 00:39:34,080
You always start at the most fragile spot because you don't want to end at a fragile spot.

495
00:39:34,081 --> 00:39:36,960
you're almost guaranteeing that you're going to break the egg.

496
00:39:37,440 --> 00:39:46,260
I'll go back and I'll try to make this even closer together than it already is.

497
00:39:46,760 --> 00:39:50,660
There's a flower in here and there's a star and there's other shapes.

498
00:39:52,020 --> 00:39:52,720
There's a heart.

499
00:39:53,780 --> 00:40:02,660
But this is the sort of egg and the sort of carving I do the night before I know I'm going to have major surgery or something

500
00:40:02,661 --> 00:40:05,220
because this takes focus,

501
00:40:05,780 --> 00:40:09,000
and you can't be thinking about anything else,

502
00:40:09,620 --> 00:40:11,160
he says as he's talking.

503
00:40:12,840 --> 00:40:15,040
The egg is a gift.

504
00:40:15,840 --> 00:40:18,300
It has drawn our interest for thousands of years

505
00:40:18,301 --> 00:40:23,120
for its simple design, delicate taste, and affordable nutrition.

506
00:40:24,960 --> 00:40:27,980
Almost always too appetizing to be left uneaten

507
00:40:27,981 --> 00:40:32,600
and often too intriguing to be left untouched.

508
00:40:32,660 --> 00:40:36,660
They're perfect in what they do.

509
00:40:36,661 --> 00:40:40,660
They're strong until you start messing with them like this.

510
00:40:40,661 --> 00:40:43,660
And sometimes they feel a little odd as an artist,

511
00:40:43,661 --> 00:40:47,660
trying to make something perfect more perfect.

512
00:40:47,661 --> 00:40:50,660
They represent life itself.

